  "id": "DrugBank_DB13762 - MONDO_6896",
  "verbalization": "Dexrabeprazole is implicated in the pathogenesis of Peptic Esophagitis through a chain of indications that link it to Camostat and subsequently to the disease. The evidence suggests that Dexrabeprazole is associated with Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD) and Duodenal Ulcer, both of which are linked to Camostat, a drug that is also indicated for Peptic Esophagitis. This indicates a potential role of Dexrabeprazole in the development of Peptic Esophagitis via its interaction with Camostat, which may modulate the gastrointestinal environment. The overlapping indications between Dexrabeprazole and Camostat suggest a possible mechanism where Dexrabeprazole could influence the pathophysiology of Peptic Esophagitis, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The evidence supports a pathway where Dexrabeprazole may contribute to the development of Peptic Esophagitis through its interaction with Camostat, highlighting a potential repurposing of Dexrabeprazole for this condition.",
